1. Nf3 f5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 g6 4. b3 Bg7 5. Bb2 Nc6 6. d4 Ne4 7. Qc1 d5 8. O-O
O-O 9. c4 e6 10. Rd1 g5 11. Ne5 Nxe5 12. dxe5 g4 13. f3 gxf3 14. exf3 Ng5 15.
f4 Ne4 16. Bxe4 fxe4 17. Nc3 c6 18. Nxe4 Rf7 19. Qc2 Bf8 20. Kh1 Be7 21. Rd2
Qf8 22. Nd6 Bxd6 23. exd6 Qxd6 24. c5 Qd8 25. Qc3 Kf8 26. Re1 Bd7 27. f5 e5 28.
Qxe5 Qg5 29. Rde2 Rd8 30. g4 Rc8 31. Qh8+ Qg8 32. Qe5 Qg5 33. Qh8+ Qg8 34. Qe5
Qg5 {(Alexander could have claimed a threefold repetition here - JS)} 35. Qd6+
Kg8 36. Qe5 {Source: CHESS, October 1956, p25} (36. Qe5 {and Alexander now
indicated that he would repeat with} Kf8 {Source: BCM, Oct 1956, p261 
"Friedman [was] apparently under the impression that a position brought about
by a different order of moves did not count for the repetition rule. That
night, Alexander (who stayed at the same hotel as his opponent), bought the
disconsolate Friedman a bottle of wine. "Never mind the wine," said the doctor,
"buy me a copy of the rules." (Heidenfeld report, BCM, Oct 1956, p262)})
1/2-1/2

