1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. Be2 O-O 8.
O-O Nc6 9. Nb3 Be6 10. f4 Qc8 11. h3 Rd8 12. Qe1 d5 13. e5 d4 14. exf6 Bxf6 {
The Scottish champion Aitken is a sound and strong player but inclined to
stereotyped openings; he decides a certain line of play is best, then sticks
to it for years. Finding that he was addicted to 12.Qe1 in this variation, I
studied it for hours before the game and unearthed this involved continuation
which sets White all sorts of traps. If White now makes the obvious move 15.
Ne4, he gets a very poor game.} 15. Ne4 $2 dxe3 16. Nxf6+ exf6 17. Bd3 Bxb3 18.
axb3 Re8 {As Black, you expect a hard struggle for equality against any
opponent in the British championship. Here, after 18 moves, I was facing a
position I had already examined at least twice in the few hours before the
game; in which I was a pawn up (and a supported passed pawn on the sixth rank).
Because I had not needed to make any calculation at all, merely draw on memory,
I had not used up one minute of my precious time as yet, whereas my opponent
had taken more than an hour. In short, the game Is virtually won already as a
result of pre­liminary study. You don't know whether to be proud or ashamed
of such a game. If you can win only one game out of fifty in top-rank chess by
such means, how­ever, it is welcome.} 19. f5 Qd8 20. Ra4 Re5 21. Kh1 Qe7 22.
Rff4 Re8 23. Qe2 ({Not} 23. Rae4 $2 Rxe4 24. Bxe4 Qxe4 25. Rxe4 Rxe4 {and
Black's rook and knight can force in the passed pawn to e1 in face of all the
Queen can do.}) 23... g5 ({Dr. Aitken pointed out after the game that I could
now have played} 23... b5 {, e.g.} 24. Bxb5 Rxb5 25. Qxb5 e2 26. Rae4 e1=Q+ {
, etc. I could hardly help winning now, anyway.}) 24. Rg4 Qd6 25. Rge4 Rxe4 26.
Bxe4 Qd2 27. Bd3 Nb4 28. Ra1 Nxd3 29. Qxd3 Qxd3 30. cxd3 Rd8 31. Rd1 e2 32. Re1
Rxd3 33. Rxe2 Rxb3 34. Kg1 h5 35. Kf1 Kf8 36. Ke1 h4 37. Kd1 Rb5 38. Rf2 Ke7
39. Kc2 a5 40. Kc3 Kd6 41. Rd2+ Rd5 42. Rf2 b5 43. b3 Kc5 44. Ra2 b4+ 45. Kc2
Kb5 {Source: Birmingham Daily Post, 16 October 1956 - BH Wood column} 0-1

