1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Qc2 e5 8. Rd1
Re8 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. e4 c6 11. b3 Qb6 12. Bb2 Nc5 13. Nxe5 Nfxe4 14. Bxe4 Bxe5
15. Bxe5 Rxe5 16. Nc3 Bg4 17. Rf1 Ne6 18. Na4 Qd4 19. Bg2 Be2 20. Rfe1 {
But the hero of the round—beautiful though unlucky— was Ritson Morry; and
if this is the first time anybody has called him beautiful he is welcome to it.
Alexander, emphatically forsaking his Leningrad Variation in favour of the
King’s Indian, had built up the overwhelming position. He now played} Re8 {
, threatening in all directions and waiting for the following “mistake.
’’ Morry played what seemed the natural move to contain the black queen}
21. Qc3 {(“but doesn’t the man see that he is losing a piece?’’), and
the trap closed with} b5 22. Rad1 $3 ({The game seems over bar the scoring: if
} 22. cxb5 cxb5 23. Qxd4 (23. Rad1 {still works - Houdini 4 Pro}) 23... Nxd4
24. Nc3 Nc2 $19 {wins the exchange}) ({and if (the prettiest variation)} 22.
Bxc6 bxa4 23. Qxd4 Nxd4 24. f4 $5 (24. Bxe8 Nf3+ 25. Kg2 Nxe1+ 26. Rxe1 Rxe8
$19 {wins a piece}) 24... Nxc6 25. fxe5 Rxe5 26. bxa4 Nd4 27. Kf2 Nc2 28. Rxe2
Rxe2+ 29. Kxe2 Nxa1 30. Kd3 {—and Black wins no material, because the Knight
is caught: but after} Kf8 31. Kc3 Ke7 32. Kb2 Kd6 33. Kxa1 Kc5 $19 {White is
helpless in the pawn ending. Alexander was still indulging these pleasant
fancies when Morry moved. He moved with just the tiniest shrug of the
shoulders as if to emphasize his complete nonchalance in this position that to
us ordinary mortals seemed so complicated. And he moved none of the things we
have discussed above with animal seriousness. He moved 22.Rad1}) {At first
Alexander also felt like shrugging his shoulders—surely there must be some
way out and the whole thing was just a straw fire. It just couldn’t be
anything else. It took quite a while before the horrible realization dawned on
him that, far from winning, he was actually losing material. Then he made the
best of a bad job:} 22... Bxd1 23. Rxe5 Rb8 (23... Rd8 $5 24. cxb5 cxb5 25.
Rxb5 Be2 26. Qxd4 Nxd4 27. Rd5 Rxd5 28. Bxd5 $16 {Houdini 4 Pro}) 24. cxb5 cxb5
25. Rxe6 (25. Rd5 $1 $16 {Houdini 4 Pro}) 25... Qxc3 26. Nxc3 fxe6 27. Nxd1 Rc8
28. Bf3 Rc2 29. Kg2 b4 {What made this magnificent defensive combination so
difficult to foresee was a sort of chain reaction. There were no loose pieces
about when the whole thing started: but capturing the rook exposed the knight,
which (a) had to guard the queen and (b) was pinned against the rook. This
exposed the queen, and the exchange of queens caught the bishop in its toils.
And what happened then? Oh yes, I almost forgot. Morry played} 30. Bc6 $4 ({
Alexander took the Bishop and scored a 1 in the tournament table. Naturally,
after} 30. Ne3 Rxa2 31. Bc6 $18 {followed by Ba4 and Nc4 White would have had
not an easy, but a certain win, despite the loss of one of his pawns.}) 30...
Rxc6 {Next day, the charming wife of one of the competitors offered to buy
Morry a pair of glasses. "You'd better save up, lady, and make it a ruddy
telescope," was the reply. Meanwhile the philosophers are still discussing
whether it is better to make a magnificent combination and lose or be the
victim of a magnificent combination and win. (Heidenfeld, BCM, Oct. 1956, page
266) Source of full score: Birmingham Daily Post, 25 September 1956 - BH Wood
column} 0-1

